{% extends "specification_user.jinja2" %}
{% block requirements %}
Create the API Specification in English. Use identifiers such as API-001, preferring existing source identifiers. Reference screen IDs and events from the preceding Screen Function Specification.

## API Inventory and Responsibilities
List each API's ID, purpose, calling screens or external systems, synchronous or asynchronous behavior, HTTP method, path, authentication, and authorization.
If methods or paths are absent from the source, label them Needs confirmation or Proposal instead of inventing confirmed details.
## Interfaces by API
Define path, query, header, and body parameters by name, meaning, type, required status, constraints, and default value.
Specify successful HTTP status codes, response fields and their types and required status, pagination, and necessary request and response examples. Label proposed examples explicitly.
## Processing and Consistency
Describe input validation, business processing, data reads and writes, transaction boundaries, idempotency, concurrency, timeouts, and retries within the source's scope.
## Errors and Acceptance Criteria
Describe failure conditions, HTTP status codes, business error codes, response bodies, caller actions, and acceptance criteria for success and failure. Mark unknown values and codes as Needs confirmation.
## Consistency and Open Questions
Show links to screen operations, matters to carry into the later Data and Exception Handling Specifications, and source references.
{% endblock %}
